# Golden questions for retrieval and answer evaluation. One JSON object per
# line; blank lines and #-comments are ignored.
#
#   id                 stable identifier, used in the miss report
#   question           what a user would actually type
#   expected_slugs     professor slug(s) a good answer should draw on (ANY = hit)
#   expected_sections  optional: section types the evidence should come from
#   reference          optional: a ground-truth answer in prose. Only the
#                      LLM-judged metrics use it (evaluation/ragas_eval), and
#                      cases without one are skipped by those metrics rather
#                      than scored zero.
#   expect_no_answer   true when the RIGHT behaviour is to refuse. Names no slugs.
#   notes              why this case exists / what it is probing
#
# HOW THIS SET WAS BUILT (matters for how much to trust it)
#
# Expectations were derived from the RAW corpus in GCS -- 867 profiles and 252
# weblinks records, keyword-searched offline -- NOT from what the retriever
# returns. Deriving them from retrieval output would guarantee high recall and
# measure nothing. Every claim in every `reference` is traceable to text in the
# profile or weblinks record of the person named.
#
# WHY SOME CASES HAVE NO REFERENCE
#
# A reference is a yardstick, so it is only written where the answer is bounded.
# For a deliberately broad question ("who works on machine learning?") dozens of
# people are correct, and a reference naming three of them would make every
# other valid professor score as a context-precision error. Those cases carry a
# wide `expected_slugs` list instead: recall@k and MRR still score them, and so
# do the judged metrics that need no reference (context_relevance, faithfulness,
# response_groundedness, context_utilization, answer_relevancy).
#
# WHY THERE ARE NO-ANSWER CASES
#
# Without them an evaluation can only reward retrieving more, so raising
# MIN_RETRIEVAL_SCORE always looks free. Each no-answer topic was checked to
# have zero support in the raw corpus. They are only meaningful at a nonzero
# floor -- at --min-score 0 nothing is ever filtered, and the report says so.
#
# COMPOSITION
#
# 60 cases: narrow topics with references, broad topics without, paraphrases
# with no term overlap, one case per section type (including the weblinks-only
# sections), role/campus filters that are expected to be hard until the
# retriever filters on metadata, conjunctive questions, and 7 no-answer cases.
{"id": "crypto-secure-computation", "question": "Which Khoury faculty work on secure multiparty computation?", "expected_slugs": ["abhi-shelat", "daniel-wichs", "zhengzhong-jin"], "expected_sections": ["research_interests", "biography"], "reference": "Abhi Shelat works on secure computation protocols, which let mutually distrusting parties with private inputs jointly compute a function while preserving privacy and correctness. Daniel Wichs researches all aspects of modern cryptography, including computing on encrypted data and the cryptographic challenges of outsourcing data and computation to the cloud.", "notes": "Narrow, strong-signal topic with a small verified answer set."}
{"id": "crypto-quantum-threat", "question": "Is anyone studying how quantum computers threaten existing cryptography?", "expected_slugs": ["cody-freitag", "zhengzhong-jin"], "expected_sections": ["biography"], "reference": "Cody Freitag, a postdoctoral research associate, studies the potential threat that quantum computers pose to the security of existing cryptographic protocols, alongside his work on the theory of cryptographic proof systems and blockchains.", "notes": "Evidence is in the biography only -- no research_interests entry says 'quantum'."}
{"id": "crypto-proof-systems", "question": "Who works on cryptographic proof systems?", "expected_slugs": ["zhengzhong-jin", "cody-freitag", "daniel-wichs"], "expected_sections": ["biography"], "reference": "Zhengzhong Jin, an assistant professor, developed a proof system that lets clients delegate computation, with applications to blockchains. Cody Freitag's research centres on the theory of cryptographic proof systems and their applications to the decentralized setting of blockchains.", "notes": "Two people, both evidenced in biography prose rather than interest lists."}
